ur text but it's pointing in the wrong  direction and if we want to solve this  in a reusable way we'd want to create a  direction where icon let's call it icon  chevron next by default it returns the  icon pointing to the right but if we  wrap it in with direction we'll get  access to that directional context and  we can return a left icon in the our  tail context so this of course changes  the game if we reference this  new-and-improved icon and set a  direction provider at the top we can  easily have a separate that works in  every language with no problem  so with these basic tools at our  disposal we wanted to investigate how  many of our components would need to be  wrapped in with direction and customized  according to that value and  unfortunately the answer was quite a lot  the deer attribute only handles  inline-block in tabular layouts and a  lot of our pages were styled in  different ways in fact even the most  basic layout characteristics like the  fact that this bucket box should be on  kind of like the far side of the page  doesn't get fixed by the deer attribute  and we'd have to manually address all of  these little style tweaks and spacings  and things like that if we didn't come  up with a better solution and we really  didn't have the resources to deal with  that so um we started investigating  automated options we found a number of  open source projects including our to  CSS flip RTL CSS CSS Janus web pack RTL  plug-in even like ah I've lost all my  slides and all of these packages came  with one major caveat they're all CSS  packages and we'd been spending the past  two years moving away from style sheets  so none of these were really helpful for  us maybe in 2014 when we'd been relying  on kind of an internal bootstrap like  framework but at this time it wasn't  really relevant I'm gonna go on a bit of  a tangent to explain Airbnb had  traditionally been Ruby on Rails  backbone sass style sheet shop but as we  move to a world of react the style  sheets we had were still associated  largely with routes  whereas our components were more feature  based so they might be shared across  multiple routes and cross multiple pages  we also had a lot of generic class names  that were really hard to track down and  grep for which made it really difficult  to determine what component required  which style sheet and which style sheets  were still in effect this was like a  particularly big one if you can't figure  out if a particular override or style is  still being used that ends up in  descending down an enormous amount of  CSS some of which is an applicable to  the page you're looking at some of it  which isn't even applicable to any page  on Airbnb and this was causing a big  performance issue all of these things  coupled together led to incident after  incident I think I sent out six  individual post mortems related to  mistaken CSS stylesheets so we started  considering other options the question  we asked was how do we style react  components while minimizing side effects  performance impact and implicit  dependencies the answer for us and I  will emphasize that it may not be for  you was to couple Styles with our  components and write our CSS in our  react components the idea of CSS and J's  had been hot around the web for a while  and there were numerous paradigms out  there already we were personally leaning  towards Khan Academy's aphrodite library  for a number of reasons ended up trying  it in our shared component library  I'll delve into the basics of how it  works with Aphrodite the most important  thing is the style sheet somewhere in  your component you want to call  Aphrodite style sheet create method with  your definitions it could be on import  it could be deferred to the first render  but that variable needs to be accessible  by your Dom elements Aphrodite notably  also handles fancy selectors things like  pseudo selectors and match media queries  and that's a feature that's built on a  pluggable